{#
  Filtering, with nothing WordPress does not already parse.

  `project_category[]` is the taxonomy's own query var. WP_Query reads it whether it
  arrives as an array from a checkbox group or as `?project_category=a,b`, sorts the
  terms and builds one IN clause — so this form needs no hook, no manager and no
  custom parameter. `orderby` is a public query var for the same reason.

  Without JavaScript this submits and the page reloads filtered. `Fetch` enhances that:
  it requests the three regions a filter changes — the count, this form and the results
  — and swaps them.

  The form is one of those regions because almost everything in it is a function of the
  current URL rather than of what the visitor last clicked: which boxes are ticked,
  which options are dead ends, how many results each one would give, whether there is
  anything to reset. Leaving it out of the response froze all of that at whatever the
  first page load said. Only the server can compute it — the counts are a query.

  `mode` is `morph`, and that is what makes a self-updating form possible at all. The
  default `replace` calls `replaceWith`, which destroys the node it is handed: the form
  the visitor is in the middle of using would be thrown away and rebuilt on every
  keystroke-equivalent, taking focus to `<body>` and taking with it the `Fetch`
  instance that is running the request. morphdom mutates the existing element instead,
  so the form node — and the component bound to it — survives while the server's answer
  is written into it.

  `selector` stays at its default of `[id]`. The response holds these three wrappers
  and nothing else, so there is no `#main` in it to match, which is what the named
  selector used to exist to avoid.

  The address bar gets the plain URL: `history` follows the element's own `action`
  rather than what was requested, so `?foehn_sections=…` never reaches it or anything a
  visitor copies out of it. That is what @studiometa/ui 1.11.0 added, and the reason
  this can use `src` at all.

  It costs a second cache file per filter combination — the fragment beside the page the
  no-JS path warms. Both are cached: `foehn_sections` is a cache-key query argument, so
  a filtered fragment comes off disk exactly like a filtered page.

  `fetch()` is called with no URL, because the component builds one itself: since 1.11.0
  it folds *every* value of a repeated field onto `src`, which is what a checkbox group
  is made of. Ticking a second series adds a second `project_category[]` to the request,
  exactly as the no-JS submit does. The fixed `foehn_sections` in `src` survives
  alongside them.

  `abort()` before each request makes the last click the one that wins. Two requests in
  flight together are otherwise a race the network settles, and the earlier answer can
  arrive last and rewrite the form under the boxes that are ticked — which now that the
  form is in the response is a visible wrong answer rather than a stale listing.
#}
{% set archive_url = fn('get_post_type_archive_link', 'project') %}
{% set filter_sections = 'project-count,project-filters,project-index' %}

<form
  method="get"
  action="{{ archive_url }}"
  class="shell filters"
  data-component="Fetch Action"
  data-option-history
  data-option-mode="morph"
  data-option-src="{{ foehn_section_url(filter_sections, archive_url) }}"
  data-on:change="() => { Fetch.abort(); Fetch.fetch() }">
  {#
    `facet()` is what makes these options a facet rather than a list: each one knows how
    many results it would give in the view being looked at, and an option that would
    give none says so instead of leaving the visitor to find out.

    The counts come from the current filters with *this* taxonomy's own filter lifted.
    Left in, picking one series would report zero for every other series and a visitor
    could never pick a second one — see Studiometa\Foehn\Query\Facets.
  #}
  <fieldset class="filters__group">
    <legend class="label label--ink">Series</legend>

    {#
      Checkboxes, and several may be ticked at once: `project_category[]` reaches
      WP_Query as an array and becomes one `IN` clause, so two series show the union of
      both. Radios would be the honest control for a single choice, and this is not one
      — narrowing to one series is what clicking a series on a project page already
      does.
    #}

    {% for option in facet('project_category') %}
      <label class="filters__option {{ option.isEmpty ? 'filters__option--empty' }}">
        <input
          type="checkbox"
          name="project_category[]"
          value="{{ option.term.slug }}"
          {{ option.active ? 'checked' }}
          {# A dead end is disabled rather than hidden: the set of filters should not
             move around as the visitor uses it. An already-checked one stays usable,
             or there would be no way to undo it. #}
          {{ option.isEmpty and not option.active ? 'disabled' }} />
        {{ option.term.name }}
        {% if option.count is not null %}
          <span class="filters__count">{{ option.count }}</span>
        {% endif %}
      </label>
    {% endfor %}
  </fieldset>

  {#
    `orderby` and `order` are both public query vars, so this pair needs no more
    configuration than the series do. The default is neither of them: with nothing asked
    for, `ThemeHooks` orders the archive by the curated sequence.
  #}
  <label class="filters__group">
    <span class="label label--ink">Sort by</span>
    <select name="orderby">
      <option value="date" {{ query_get('orderby', 'date') == 'date' ? 'selected' }}>Date</option>
      <option value="title" {{ query_get('orderby') == 'title' ? 'selected' }}>Title</option>
    </select>
  </label>

  {# Lowercase, because the page cache keys the value and `DESC` would be a second
     spelling of the same page. WordPress reads either. #}
  <label class="filters__group">
    <span class="label label--ink">Order</span>
    <select name="order">
      <option value="desc" {{ query_get('order', 'desc')|lower == 'desc' ? 'selected' }}>Descending</option>
      <option value="asc" {{ query_get('order')|lower == 'asc' ? 'selected' }}>Ascending</option>
    </select>
  </label>

  {#
    The one filter here that WordPress will not read from a URL by itself.
    `posts_per_page` is a private query var — a visitor who can set it can ask for every
    post on the site — so `QueryFiltersHook` makes it public against the allowlist in
    `app/query-filters.config.php`, and a value outside that list is refused rather than
    clamped.
  #}
  <label class="filters__group">
    <span class="label label--ink">Per page</span>
    <select name="posts_per_page">
      {% for size in [3, 6, 12] %}
        <option value="{{ size }}" {{ query_get('posts_per_page', 3) == size ? 'selected' }}>{{ size }}</option>
      {% endfor %}
    </select>
  </label>

  {#
    The no-JS path, and the way out of a filter. Both are plain HTML.

    Reset appears only when there is something to reset, which is a thing this form can
    say again now that the server re-renders it: the link is a function of the query,
    and until the form was a section it would have been rendered once on a bare archive
    and never seen afterwards.

    It fetches the same three regions as the form, against the cleared URL. The empty
    form in that response is what unticks the boxes — a reset that only swapped the
    results would leave every filter looking as if it were still applied.
  #}
  <div class="filters__actions">
    <button type="submit">Filter</button>
    {% if query_all() is not empty %}
      <a
        href="{{ query_url_clear() }}"
        data-component="Fetch"
        data-option-history
        data-option-mode="morph"
        data-option-src="{{ foehn_section_url(filter_sections, query_url_clear()) }}">
        Reset
      </a>
    {% endif %}
  </div>
</form>
